  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nA daily chance of showers and thunderstorms is in the forecast \ntoday and Friday. With this, a few strong to severe thunderstorms \nare possible during the afternoon and evening both days, generally\neast of Billings.\n\nThe area remains in southwest flow aloft today, with troughing \nover the Pac NW and ridging over the eastern US. Southeasterly \nwinds over the east are transporting in moisture and instability, \nwith dewpoint temps in the 50s, and ML CAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg over\nthe northeast. As we go through the afternoon and evening, a weak\nshortwave is expected to cross the region, bringing isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to the area, with a couple \ndifferent areas of focus for stronger thunderstorms. Across the \nfar east where instability is greatest, a few strong to severe \nthunderstorms are possible later this afternoon, with initiation \noccurring just inside the MT border, or east into the Dakotas, \ndepending on which model you look at. The other area of focus is \nthe Bighorn/Pryor mountains, where thunderstorms look to develop \nthis afternoon, lifting north and east through the evening. A few \nstorms could be strong to potentially severe with this activity as\nwell. That being said, with deep layer shear of only 20-30kts, \nconvection could be more pulsey in nature. The main threats with\nthunderstorms are large hail, strong winds and brief heavy rain. \nSPC has a slight risk for severe thunderstorms near the Dakotas \nborder, with a marginal risk for areas east of Billings. Look for \nconvection to gradually decrease this evening, with isolated to \nwidely scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms continuing \nover the far east overnight into early Friday.\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are forecast \nacross the area during the afternoon and evening again on Friday\nas another shortwave crosses the region. Modest instability and\nshear will bring the threat of a few strong to severe\nthunderstorms for areas generally east of Billings. SPC currently\nhas a marginal risk of severe thunderstorms over southeast MT.\nHail, strong winds and brief heavy rain are once again the main\nthreats.\n\nConditions trend hotter and drier for Independence Day into \nSunday as ridging builds over the region. High temperatures in the\n80s to lower 90s can be expected. By Sunday evening, a shortwave\nmoving in from the west could bring a chance for\nshowers/thunderstorms (15-30%) for western and central areas. Hot\ntemperatures in the 80s to 90s are forecast to continue through at\nleast Wednesday as higher heights remain over the region. That\nbeing said, moisture moving into the region under the ridge\nfrom the southeast and weak shortwaves passing through at times \nwill keep daily shower/thunderstorm chances in the forecast. One \nthing to watch would be how warm temperatures aloft get. The warm\ntemperatures aloft tend to inhibit convection in our region,\nkeeping a mid-level cap in place. STP",
